WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.630
Some of the most memorable moments in recent
TV history weren't even planned, but were

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:08.720
instead born from inspired, improvised moments
between gifted actors.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.890
They may have felt too perfectly placed to
be off-the-cuff, but here are a few genuinely

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:16.900
unscripted exchanges that took viewers — and
producers — totally by surprise.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:21.730
And we're not making this one up on the spot:
there are definite spoilers ahead.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.300
Stormborn smooch

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:28.169
Game of Thrones is a very expensive, obsessively
examined show, so it makes sense to assume

00:00:28.169 --> 00:00:30.449
every moment must be scripted to the letter.

00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:34.190
However, as it turns out, there's some room
for flexibility on the series.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:39.010
In the season 7 episode "Stormborn," Yara
Greyjoy and Ellaria Sand get to know each

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.400
other a little better — before being interrupted
by an attack …

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.200
"Seems like you don't need a protector."

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:56.640
Actress Gemma Whelan, who plays Yara, revealed
after the episode aired that the kiss wasn't

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.899
scripted, saying that it, quote, "just seemed
like something we should do."

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:02.899
Moira knows best

00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:08.050
Many fans were left heartbroken when Oliver's
mother Moira bit the dust on The CW's Arrow.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:12.340
Moira sacrificed her life for her children,
and moments before getting a sword through

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.890
the chest, she pleaded with her daughter to
look away ...

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.580
"Thea, I love you!

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:19.680
Close your eyes, baby!"

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:21.340
"No!"

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.340
Actress Susanna Thompson later revealed that
plea was actually improvised: she came up

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:29.320
with the "close your eyes" comment as a way
to bridge the gap between Moira telling Slade

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.040
to let her children live and her actual death.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:37.070
Executive producer Marc Guggenheim ended up
loving the line, so it made the final cut.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:38.740
Poppins improv

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.861
Happy Endings was a joke machine in its three
seasons on ABC.

00:01:41.861 --> 00:01:46.960
With a naturally hilarious cast, it makes
sense some of those jokes would be improvised

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.810
— including a small but fun moment featuring
a Mary Poppins reference from Jane, played

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:52.800
by Eliza Coupe...

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.520
"Why is it so freezing in here?"

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:58.180
"I don't know, why don't you ask 'tuppence
a bag'?"

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:02.610
Coupe was referencing her husband, played
by Damon Wayans Jr., whose cologne attracts

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:03.610
pigeons.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:07.910
The actress was surprised to see that people
on Twitter noticed and appreciated the relatively

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:12.080
obscure joke, which is a reference to the
"Feed the Birds" scene in the Disney classic.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.540
"Feed the birds.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.780
Tuppence a bag."

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:17.780
Off on the right foot

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:21.920
Arthur was never a great friend to Merlin
while starring on the character's namesake

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.940
BBC One series, but the two did have fun messing
with each other on the show — just as the

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:28.310
actors did behind the scenes.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:32.550
According to actor Bradley James, the scene
in which his Arthur rubs his foot on Merlin's

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:38.250
face in season one after a sweet bonding conversation
was actually just him messing with actor Colin

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.750
Morgan, but it ended up making it into the
final cut.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:44.810
James revealed on the DVD commentary that
that was his "favorite bit" of the episode,

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:47.830
but that he didn't do it in rehearsals.

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:49.190
Cry baby

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:53.010
There were a lot of heartbreaking moments
in Breaking Bad over the years, but one of

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:57.310
the biggest tearjerkers came when Walt's baby
daughter Holly started calling out for her

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.320
mother after her father abducted her.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:02.480
"Won't that be nice, Holly?"

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:04.520
"Mama!

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:06.880
Mama.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:08.760
Mama."

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.300
But that's not how the scene was planned.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.500
During the bathroom scene, the little actress
saw her real mother just a few feet away,

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.260
and ended up calling out to her.

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:21.410
Credit goes to actor Bryan Cranston for rolling
with it, because the moment ended up being

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:23.000
an instant classic.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.200
Fruit of his labor

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:29.591
Even if you only watched Psych intermittently,
you know how important pineapples were to

00:03:29.591 --> 00:03:30.591
the show.

00:03:30.591 --> 00:03:35.069
As it turns out, the whole thing started thanks
to an improvisation from Psych star James

00:03:35.069 --> 00:03:35.880
Roday.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:36.520
"Ooh!

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.599
Should I slice this up for the road?"

00:03:39.599 --> 00:03:44.170
Roday was so enthusiastic about the joke that
the producers felt like he just had to keep

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:48.599
it in, and it spiraled out from there: One
of the tropical fruits appeared in almost

00:03:48.599 --> 00:03:53.640
every episode of the series, in addition to
featuring prominently in its marketing over

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:54.640
the years.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:55.890
Thanks for watching!

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:58.720
